  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 231 PM MDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nThursday through Friday:\n\nSynoptically, the upper level trough will begin moving into the Tri-\nState area Thursday morning. The majority of guidance indicates that \nthe center of the trough will track to the southeast of the County \nWarning Area (CWA). By Friday, the system is expected to move out of \nthe CWA, leaving our upper level flow mainly from the northwest to \nnorth. \n\nAt the surface, guidance indicates a boundary will move through from \nthe northwest, as well as a potential surface low to the southeast. \nExpect breezy winds Thursday morning. Gusts of 30-45 mph are \ncurrently forecast, with higher gusts expected across the Colorado \ncounties as well as areas south of I-70. Highs on Thursday and \nFriday are forecast to be in the 60s to lower 70s. \n\nThe main hazard associated with this system will be heavy rainfall. \nStorm total precipitation guidance shows 1-4 inches across the area \nby Friday night into Saturday morning, with the majority of guidance \nagreeing the higher amounts staying south of the Nebraska border. \nHowever, some models, such as the ICON and GDPS, show a heavy \nprecipitation band of 4-5 inches right along the eastern border of \nthe CWA. The LREF shows a 10-25% probability of exceeding 3 inches \nby Friday night. Ultimately, these totals will depend heavily on the \ntrack of the upper level trough and potential surface low set up. A \nfarther north track would shift the the higher precipitation amounts \nnorthward. A flood watch remains in effect for the CWA through \nThursday afternoon. \n\n\nSaturday through Monday: \n\nSynoptically, for the weekend into the early next week, upper level \nflow returns predominantly for the northwest as the jet stream \nremains near the US/Can border. Certain long range models, such as \nthe GFS, depict an upper level shortwave detaching from the main jet \nand moving through the area Saturday afternoon. The ECMWF and \nCanadian-NH models show a weaker, more subtle shortwave feature. The \nProbabilities of Precipitation (PoPs) for the weekend remain under \n10%. If this feature becomes strong then, it could increase our \nprecipitation chances. \n\nHigh temperatures though the weekend are forecast to remain below 80 \ndegrees. Saturday and Monday highs will be in the low to mid 70s \nacross the Tri-State area, with a brief \"cooldown\" into the 60s on \nSunday. Winds throughout the latter half of the long term period \nlook fairly breezy, with Saturday having the highest forecast gusts \nin the 20-30 mph range as yet another boundary passes through.",
